{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Harass the Midianites, etc.</b> One needs to know why He doubled <b>harass and strike them</b>, and our Sages of blessed memory sensed this and said (Numbers Rabbah 21) that the intention here is to say that even though HaShem commanded (Deuteronomy 20:10) ״כִּי תִקְרַב אֶל עִיר וְגוֹ׳ לֹא תַשְׁחִית אֶת עֵצָהּ״ etc., this is a light and despised nation — [nevertheless] destroy its trees etc., end. And one needs to know what vengeance this is upon Midian; on the contrary, they should have fulfilled (Deuteronomy 20:14) ״וְאָכַלְתָּ אֶת שְׁלַל אוֹיְבֶיךָ״, for this would be for them a greater vengeance and craving of soul, and similar to this we find in the Torah's curses (Leviticus 26:16) ״וַאֲכָלֻהוּ אוֹיְבֵיכֶם״." "<b>Further</b>, one needs to know the verse's saying <b>כִּי צֹרְרִים</b> ('for they are enemies') — why was it necessary to give a reason? And according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory, who said that it comes to say: destroy its trees etc., we have grounds to say that it comes to give a reason for nullifying the commandment of ״לֹא תַשְׁחִית״ etc. in this place, כִּי צֹרְרִים. And in the Tanhuma they said, and this is their language: ״צָרוֹר״ etc. — why? ״כִּי צֹרְרִים״ etc. — from here they said: if one comes to kill you etc. And according to the words of the Tanhuma, the verse comes to inform you of the law regarding one who comes etc., and this is the way of derash." "<b>And it appears</b> to explain, by raising the question of why HaShem commanded this commandment not in its proper time, for we find that afterward HaShem needed to command, saying (Numbers 31:2) ״נְקֹם נִקְמַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵאֵת הַמִּדְיָנִים״ — from this you learn that this statement was not at the time of the vengeance." "<b>Indeed</b>, HaShem intended in this statement for the rectification of Israel, because whoever tastes the flavor of the natural sin that dominates a person against his will — even if it is not his conscious intent but only in the aspect of the one who thinks about it — it is difficult to separate it from him, as I elaborated on this detail in parashat Acharei Mot (Leviticus 18:3) on the verse ״כְּמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ etc. And as long as the craving for the thing and its desire has not been separated from the sinner, he is deprived of atonement, as I explained in parashat Bereshit in what the verse says (Genesis 4:7) ״וְאֵלֶיךָ תְּשׁוּקָתוֹ וְאַתָּה תִּמְשָׁל בּוֹ״. And in our case, Israel tasted the sweetness of licentiousness — some in deed, some in thought — and they also cleaved to Peor, as it is written (Numbers 25:3): ״וַיִּצָּמֶד יִשְׂרָאֵל לְבַעַל פְּעוֹר״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 20) that they became like a bracelet etc., end. And HaShem desired to heal them with the sound counsel of HaShem in order to remove the plague from upon them — for you will find that the plague was in Israel until all who had cleaved to Baal Peor were consumed and finished, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:3): ״כִּי כָל הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר הָלַךְ אַחֲרֵי בַעַל פְּעוֹר הִשְׁמִידוֹ ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ מִקִּרְבֶּךָ״. And you must say that He is not speaking of the twenty-four thousand about whom the verse says (Numbers 25:9) ״וַיִּהְיוּ הַמֵּתִים״ etc. ״אַרְבָּעָה״ etc., for those were of the tribe of Shimon and died because they sought to lay hands on Pinchas, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 20), and the verse says ״אֲשֶׁר הָלַךְ אַחֲרֵי בַעַל פְּעוֹר הִשְׁמִידוֹ״ etc. — from this you learn that He destroyed also all those who committed harlotry, in addition to the twenty-four thousand. Also, from what the verse says here, ״הַמֻּכָּה בְיוֹם הַמַּגֵּפָה עַל דְּבַר פְּעוֹר״, this indicates that those who died of Israel were only those who died on account of Pinchas, because they sought to lay hands on him." "<b>And although</b> we understand that the primary cause that brought about the deed of Pinchas was nothing but the harlotry of Zimri — for Pinchas did not kill Zimri for idol worship — nevertheless, the plague was surely dominating those who committed harlotry. And although the verse says ״וַיְכַפֵּר עַל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, meaning that not all the people were consumed — some because of the thought of idol worship, some because of the licentiousness — HaShem never relented concerning those who committed harlotry. And HaShem counseled and said ״צָרוֹר אֶת״ etc.; the intention in this is to precede hatred of those who cause sin, and also to render bitter the sweet and the good that comes from them, to destroy every desirable tree and every sweet spring and every good that comes from them, because they caused them to do evil. And this is along the lines of what he said (Psalms 139:21) ״הֲלֹא מְשַׂנְאֶיךָ ה׳ אֶשְׂנָא״ — meaning, ״מְשַׂנְאֶיךָ״ ('those who hate You') as if it said מַשְׂנִיאֶיךָ ('those who cause You to be hated'), who cause hatred of the chosen one toward the good, in the manner that they of blessed memory interpreted (Shabbat 114a) regarding the verse ״כָּל מְשַׂנְאַי אָהֲבוּ מָוֶת״ — do not read it [thus] etc. And through the medium of this thought, the desire for transgression will move away from them and become alienated to them, for through the transgression those around become hated, and a divine nature will be born in them. And this trait is a distinctive trait for one who desires life, and through this the previous sin will be atoned for — in the thought of idol worship and also in the snare of the desire for adultery — and there will be no plague among them." "<b>And this</b> is the meaning of the verse here: <b>Harass the Midianites</b> — meaning, they shall be for you in the category of a foe and enemy, to hate them and to hate all who intermingle from among them, to destroy their counsel and to ruin their sources. This is in exchange for what they desired against them, and this is one of the repairs of repentance. And He said <b>צָרוֹר</b> in the present tense — meaning, intensify the hatred toward them until it becomes a fence, so that the nature of enmity becomes fixed in them. And He said <b>and you shall strike them</b> — meaning, wait for a day of vengeance when you will strike them; therefore He did not say ״strike them״ as an imperative, because the time is not now. And He said <b>for they are adversaries</b> etc. — the intention here is to say that the matter is conditional: the hatred and enmity you shall have toward them is not because they caused a falling among Israel, for if so, the purpose of repairing the sin would be lost. Rather, <b>for they are adversaries</b> etc. <b>on account of Peor</b>, in that they caused you to sin through idolatry, <b>and on account of Cozbi</b> etc., in that they caused you to stumble through licentiousness. And the reason He attached this detail to Cozbi alone is because she was a king's daughter and He mentioned her greatness; also because her act of licentiousness was public, and it is possible that all the promiscuous acts were prompted by her mouth." "<b>Further</b>, it is possible that to this the verse intended in the phrase <b>a daughter of a prince of Midian</b>, which was unnecessary, since He had already said nearby ״Cozbi daughter of Zur״ etc. Rather, it comes to say that because she was a king's daughter, the entire nation would be seized on her account. And examine closely: He said <b>their sister</b> — to say that even though her sortie originated from Moab, ״their sister״ means their sister in counsel, for she went forth by the counsel of Midian, and the matter will be attributed to all of them. And His saying <b>the stricken one</b> — to say that she surrendered her life for them like a brother for his brother. And His saying <b>on the day of the plague</b> etc. — meaning, the plague that came on account of Peor. From this you learn that on that very day HaShem struck those who went after Peor, apart from the twenty-four thousand that the verse mentions, who were all from the tribe of Simeon and died because of Phinehas. And the primary intention of the verse is to arouse in them a natural hatred, for they were the cause of kindling HaShem's anger against the people. And one should not err by thinking that the intention here is that they should hate them because of the plague, since He already said ״for they are adversaries״ etc. — that they did not act as adversaries except on account of Peor etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:56.765360295Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}